<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg</h1><p class="st-meta">982 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Saverne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Alsatian church principality whose bishops lost their cathedral city to its burghers in 1262 but kept a territory on both banks of the Rhine until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a> had been a bishopric since late Roman times. In 982 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Otto II</a> granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> extensive rights over the city, and later Ottonian and Salian grants added counties and estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a>, making the bishops princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> began the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> cathedral under Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-i-of-strasbourg">Werner</a> in 1015. In 1262 the citizens defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-of-geroldseck">Bishop Walter of Geroldseck</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hausbergen">Hausbergen</a> and the city became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Free Imperial City of Strasbourg</a>, so the bishops governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne">Saverne</a>. Their lands lay in scattered pieces across lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a> in 1681 the left-bank lands came under French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and a line of Rohan cardinals held the see. The Revolution abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, and the right-bank remainder was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1803.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-strasbourg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg" width="320" height="311"><figcaption>Image: OwenBlacker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishopric_of_Strasbourg_1547.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>982 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saverne</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Werner of Habsburg, Walter of Geroldseck, Louis René Édouard de Rohan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France, Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bishopric of Strasbourg, Hochstift Straßburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
